{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>But the soul that… and his uncleanness…</b> The Sages interpreted this verse and the one after it as referring to uncleanness of the body in Torat Kohanim and in Zevachim (Zevachim 43a). They said: any verse that Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi did not interpret… he did not interpret. Thus said Rabbi Yitzchak bar Avdimi: since the verse opens in feminine language and concludes in feminine language, with masculine language in the middle, it is speaking of uncleanness of the body — end of quote. And Rashi explained: 'since…' — meaning the verse 'נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תִגַּע,' which is the second verse, where it is necessarily the case to say that he is speaking of uncleanness of the body, even though he changed to masculine in the middle. Also in the first verse, 'וְהַנֶּפֶשׁ,' do not wonder that it first spoke in feminine language — 'וְהַנֶּפֶשׁ' — and at the end 'וְנִכְרְתָה,' and in the middle 'וְטֻמְאָתוֹ' — for the intention is not about the flesh but about the body — end of quote. His words, may his memory be for blessing, were effective in showing that one need not infer from the change to masculine in the middle that the verse intends to speak of the uncleanness of flesh; rather, to prove that it is speaking of uncleanness of the body is from the plain implication of the verse, for it appears that the laws of the uncleanness of flesh have already been completed, as Rashi explained there in Zevachim." "Further, in Torat Kohanim they learned a gezerah shavah of 'טֻמְאָתוֹ' 'טֻמְאָתוֹ' — the latter mentioned regarding entry into the Sanctuary (Numbers 19:13). But I set my mind to [understand] why the verse changed in these two verses: in one verse it changed to write 'וְטֻמְאָתוֹ' and thereby provided the basis for learning a gezerah shavah — whereas it should have written 'וְטֻמְאָתָהּ' and then we would not have needed a gezerah shavah. Also in the second verse, why did it not use straightforward language throughout the verse in a single order?" "<b>And it appears</b> that the reason for its saying <b>and his uncleanness is upon him</b> comes to signal another matter: that even though the seven days of impurity for corpse-impurity have passed, or sunset has arrived for creeping-creature impurity, as long as he has not immersed, he is still in his impurity. And you should know that this impurity is not within the interior of the soul, but is rather a covering of impurity that overlies the body from the outside and not inside. From this perspective immersion is effective for him to remove the barrier of impurity — which would not be effective for him during the ongoing days of impurity, when the impurity is also within. And this is the measurement of the verse: <b>a soul that eats…</b> <b>and his uncleanness is upon him</b> — meaning, not only 'her uncleanness is upon her,' that is, during the seven days for corpse-impurity and during the period of his contact for creeping-creature impurity, but even his uncleanness of body alone — that is, the seven days of impurity have already passed for him and he lacks nothing but immersion to remove it from his body — even so <b>she shall be cut off.</b>" "And the reason for its using masculine language in the middle in the second verse — even though there is no error in this, it should not have said anything but a single order throughout the verse. And it appears that the reason for its saying <b>and a soul when it touches</b> in feminine language, and not saying 'אִישׁ כִּי יִגַּע' in masculine language, is that it intends to say that he must touch it with awareness and not unknowingly, for he is not liable to excision except with knowledge of the impurity. And likewise its saying in one verse 'וְהַנֶּפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר תֹּאכַל' indicates that the eating of the flesh was with awareness, for the word נֶפֶשׁ points to sensation and to precluding lack of awareness. And for this reason you will find that it says in the section of the unwitting sins (Leviticus 4–5) 'וְנֶפֶשׁ,' to say: the reason that obligates a sin-offering and a guilt-offering for an unwitting act is because it is a נֶפֶשׁ, which is the aspect of awareness, knowledge, and inadvertence — therefore it becomes liable to a sacrifice. And in every case the term נֶפֶשׁ indicates the aspect of negating feigned ignorance. Therefore it says in both verses 'נֶפֶשׁ כִּי תִגַּע' — behold, knowledge of the touching; 'וְהַנֶּפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר תֹּאכַל' — behold, knowledge of the food. From this point on there is no place for the difficulty of why it used masculine language in the middle, for there is no need to alter the usual order of using masculine language for the one performing the action. And the reason for its saying 'וְנִכְרְתָה הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַהִיא' also comes to teach us a novelty, that even the sinning soul itself dies, and not the body alone." "<b>And besides</b> the approach of the Sages, may their memory be for blessing, it appears possible to resolve the duplication of verses according to what they said in Zevachim folio 106 in the Mishnah: an impure person who ate — whether impure sacrifices or pure sacrifices — is liable; and Rabbi Yosei HaGelili says: an impure person who ate impure [sacrifices] is exempt. And they said in the Gemara (Zevachim 108a) — in every case where the body first became impure and afterward the flesh became impure, everyone agrees he is liable. Therefore it says two verses: the first — 'אֲשֶׁר תֹּאכַל בָּשָׂר מִזֶּבַח הַשְּׁלָמִים' — this refers to an impure person who eats the flesh of pure shelamim; and the second says 'וְאָכַל מִבְּשַׂר זֶבַח' etc. — this corresponds to an impure person who eats of the flesh of shelamim that became impure. And perhaps it was for this reason that the verse was precise in saying in one verse <b>she eats flesh from the sacrifice of shelamim</b> — the flesh is of the sacrifice of shelamim, which is still at its level of purity, having not become impure; while the second says <b>from the flesh of the sacrifice of shelamim</b> — meaning, of the category of flesh of shelamim, even though now it is no longer flesh of shelamim in which the designation of eating applies; and this is its saying 'from flesh' upon which the name 'sacrifice of shelamim' still rests — for it did not connect the flesh with the mention of 'sacrifice of shelamim' to hint that it is not in the category of ordinary pure shelamim. Examine closely, and do not let your heart turn to err after the mem in a different manner."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:17.709099527Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}